---
props {
  productId: string
}
---

{* {$let} belongs in the BODY. In frontmatter it is an unrecognised statement
   and is silently discarded, so these two lines used to emit nothing. *}
{$let loading = false}
{$let success = false}

{*
  The server renders this button in its RESTING state, and that is all it can
  do on its own:

  - loading and success are client state, so they are null here. The two
    conditional classes are simply absent from the output, disabled={} is
    omitted (an empty expression drops the attribute), and aria-busy is
    written as a literal "false".
  - The label below is an {if} chain, which is resolved once at render time.
    It always emits "Add to Cart". A host runtime that swaps the label has to
    replace the text itself; the {if} will not re-run.
  - The id is published as data-product-id rather than being read out of the
    on:click string. An ordinary attribute interpolates; an on: value does not.
*}
<button
  class={
    "btn",
    "btn--primary",
    "btn--add-to-cart",
    "btn--loading": loading,
    "btn--success": success
  }
  data-product-id="${ productId }"
  disabled={loading}
  aria-busy="false"
  aria-label="Add to cart"
  on:click="addToCart"
>
  {if loading}
    Adding...
  {else if success}
    Added!
  {else}
    Add to Cart
  {/if}
</button>

<style>
.btn--add-to-cart {
  width: 100%;
  justify-content: center;
  transition: background 0.15s, transform 0.1s;
}
.btn--loading {
  opacity: 0.7;
  cursor: not-allowed;
}
.btn--success {
  background: #15803d;
}
.btn--success:hover {
  background: #166534;
}
</style>
